---

### The most likely cause

This led me to a deep rabbit hole, tbh. I believe this is due to the [CVO render command](https://github.com/openshift/cluster-version-operator/blob/main/cmd/cluster-version-operator/render.go), which is used during a cluster installation, very early during bootstrapping. It does not respect the delete annotation, nor does it have a special logic for its rendering command to bypass this, and it creates the CRB during installation-bootstrap time. The delete annotation deletes stuff only during cluster upgrades. Thus, the CRB gets left in the cluster after an installation. This is not acceptable. The delete annotation's purpose is to enable developers to stage the deletion of manifests to ensure no leftover objects are left in the cluster after a period of time. This present state does not allow the CVO to utilize the annotation in such a way for its manifests. Nor the CVO has smarter rendering for its manifests for the bootstrap stage. The CRB would get applied for all freshly installed clusters, and we could never delete the CRB manifest file.

[This does not affect other similar components, to my understanding. This is strictly related to the very early bootstrapping of the cluster, where individual bootstrap components use their own render commands to render their bootstrap manifests](https://github.com/openshift/installer/blob/3a35f919f5f22700746e74831265b2ccc42ce20a/data/data/bootstrap/files/usr/local/bin/bootkube.sh.template), [which are then applied.](https://github.com/openshift/cluster-bootstrap/blob/b23c6ce3df43aed15158e999239694ec75371f18/pkg/start/start.go#L142)

---

### Proposal

My proposal is to keep the role binding for the default service account, remove the proposed delete annotation, remove its `kubernetes.io/description` annotation, move it to the `0000_00_cluster-version-operator_03_` runlevel to ensure correctness, and create a new separate issue for the problem: the default service account in the CVO namespace should not have cluster-admin permissions. Are you fine with such an outcome to your PR and to your assigned bug?

The bug's title and the PR's title describe the goal of having dedicated service accounts. We would have achieved that. Unfortunately, this newly found bug makes the next step of removing the extra permission from the default SA more difficult.

@DavidHurta I'm thinking that how the `upgrade-into-change` test works is that after this PR is merged, we can open a new one without the `cluster-version-operator` CRB, since it will be upgrading into a change where no pod service accounts are changing. So, it shouldn't fail... 

I'm assuming the change comes from the PR branch and the current comes from default branch. :) 

Anyway, I created the issue --> https://redhat.atlassian.net/browse/OCPBUGS-80925 .
